story test:
  stat three = 3
  event start:
    # Test 1
    # All body text is treated as markdown: each source line is a separate
    # logical line. Consecutive lines are joined into one paragraph (space separator).
    # Quotes in body text are preserved as literal characters.
    # Expected: "This a poem demonstrating how new lines work in three formats."
    "This a poem
    demonstrating how new lines work in three formats."

    # Test 2
    # Unquoted prose: consecutive lines joined into one paragraph with spaces.
    # Both CLI and JS: This is the second of the 3 formats, of course this is unquoted.
    This is the second
    of the {{three}} formats, of course
    this is unquoted.

    # Test 3
    # Quoted strings: consecutive quoted lines joined into one paragraph.
    # Both CLI and JS: "And at last we have" "a haiku where all lines have" "in fact been quoted."
    "And at last we have"
    "a haiku where all lines have"
    "in fact been quoted."

    # Test 4
    # Escaped quotes in unquoted text: \" produces a literal " character.
    # Quotes in body text are always preserved (not stripped).
    # \n produces a real newline in the output.
    # Expected: "But what if", you ask, "I wanted my string to start wrapped in double-quotes?"
    \"But what if", you ask,
    \"I wanted my string to start
    wrapped in double-quotes?"

    # Test 5
    # Quoted text in a paragraph: quotes are PRESERVED (not stripped) in body text.
    # "Or I were to add" → "Or I were to add" (quotes preserved)
    # "text" → "text" (quotes preserved)
    # a markdown stanza? → a markdown stanza? (bare unquoted, no quotes)
    # Expected: "Or I were to add" "text" before the first line of a markdown stanza?
    "Or I were to add"
    "text" before the first line of
    a markdown stanza?

    # Test 6
    # Blank-line-separated paragraphs: each stanza becomes a separate paragraph.
    # Expected:
    # Or if I did a
    #
    # stanza where each line was new
    #
    # line separated?
    # Both CLI and JS: blank lines between source stanzas produce separate
    # paragraphs, displayed with a blank line between them.
    Or if I did a

    stanza where each line was new

    line separated?

    # Test 7
    # Escape sequences in quoted strings: \n is a real newline.
    # The string is single-line in source but contains newlines at runtime.
    # Expected:
    # "Or what if it was
    # all on one quoted line but
    # using escape chars?"
    "Or what if it was\nall on one quoted line but\nusing escape chars?"

    # Test 8
    # Escape sequences in unquoted text: \n is now a real newline.
    # This was previously broken — unquoted text did not process escapes.
    # Expected:
    # Or what if it was
    # all on one unquoted line
    # using escape chars?
    Or what if it was\nall on one unquoted line \nusing escape chars?
